{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the polynomial \\(P(x)\\):\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\(P(x)\\) as:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + 23x^{23} + 22x^{25} + 21x^{22} + 20x^{26} + \\cdots + 2x^{47} + 1x^{48}.\n\\]\n\nWe notice that \\(P(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 48. To find the roots of \\(P(x)\\), we need to consider the symmetry and properties of the polynomial. Let's consider the polynomial evaluated at \\(x\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\):\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = 24 \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24-j} + \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{24}{x^{24}} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\frac{x^j + x^{48-2j}}{x^{48}} \\right) = \\frac{24}{x^{24}} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\frac{x^j + x^{48-2j}}{x^{48}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(x^{48}\\) gives:\n\n\\[\nx^{48} P\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) (x^{24-j} + x^{24+j}).\n\\]\n\nWe observe that \\(P(x) = x^{48} P\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\), which implies that if \\(z\\) is a root of \\(P(x)\\), then \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) is also a root of \\(P(x)\\). This symmetry suggests that the roots come in reciprocal pairs. Since the polynomial is of degree 48, there are 24 pairs of roots \\(z\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1379,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,2661,3491,11,582,1191,553,41018,279,47311,17767,47,2075,10699,7731,59,9640,47,2075,8,284,220,17,19,87,47822,17,19,92,488,1124,1242,15159,73,28,16,92,47822,17,18,92,320,17,19,13333,8,1124,2359,7,856,47822,17,19,13333,92,488,856,47822,17,19,28735,92,1124,1291,4292,59,2533,1654,646,181