17,19,13333,92,10,87,47822,17,19,28735,11035,1291,568,14085,10061,400,89,62,16,11,1147,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,1147,1710,3,387,279,12460,17516,315,400,47,2075,15087,11,323,1077,400,89,4698,61,17,24239,4698,35093,15159,74,92,72,3,369,400,74,28,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,54876,1380,400,72,34433,26888,19999,16,31716,11,323,400,64,4698,3,323,400,65,4698,3,525,1931,5109,13,7379,198,59,26056,1242,15159,74,28,16,92,47822,81,52398,65,4698,91,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $$P(x)=24x^{24}+\\sum_{j=1}^{23}(24-j)\\left(x^{24-j}+x^{24+j}\\right).$$Let $z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_r$ be the distinct zeros of $P(x)$, and let $z_k^2=a_k+b_{k}i$ for $k=1, 2, \\ldots, r$, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$, and $a_k$ and $b_k$ are real numbers. Find\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{r}|b_k|.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e59f3abed445e65fd2d8bc0a6d3555b1672293de030c3fa08c20b4900627ecf"},"seconds":24.990069150924683,"seed":1111962443628308269,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the polynomial \\(P(x)\\):\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\(P(x)\\) as:\n\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + 23x^{23} + 22x^{25} + 21x^{22} + 20x^{26} + \\cdots + 2x^{47} + 1x^{48}.\n\\]\n\nWe notice that \\(P(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 48. To find the roots of \\(P(x)\\), we need to consider the symmetry and properties of the polynomial. Let's consider the polynomial evaluated at \\(x\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{x}\\):\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = 24 \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24-j} + \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nP\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = \\frac{24}{x^{24}} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\frac{x^j + x^{48-2j}}{x^{48}} \\right) = \\frac{24}{x^{24}} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( \\frac{x^j + x^{48-2j}}{x^{48}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(x^{48}\\) gives:\n\n\\[\nx^{48} P\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) (x^{24-j} + x^{24+j}).\n\\]\n\nWe observe that \\(P(x) = x^{48} P\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\), which implies that if \\(z\\) is a root of \\(P(x)\\), then \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\) is also a root of \\(P(x)\\). This symmetry suggests that the roots come in reciprocal pairs. Since the polynomial is of degree 48, there are 24 pairs of roots \\(z\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{z}\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine the nature of the roots. The polynomial \\(P(x)\\) is real and symmetric, and the roots must either be real or come in complex conjugate pairs. Since the polynomial is of even degree and the coefficients are real, the complex roots must appear in conjugate pairs. Each pair \\((z, \\frac{1}{z})\\) will contribute to the sum \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k|\\).\n\nFor a root \\(z = a + bi\\) (where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers), the corresponding reciprocal root \\(\\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{1}{a+bi} = \\frac{a-bi}{a^2+b^2}\\) will have the same magnitude for the imaginary part. Therefore, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots will be twice the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots in the first quadrant.\n\nThe polynomial \\(P(x)\\) is symmetric and of degree 48, so the roots are symmetrically distributed on the unit circle in the complex plane. The imaginary parts of the roots in the first quadrant will sum to the same value as the imaginary parts in the other quadrants. Since the polynomial is of even degree and the roots are symmetric, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of all roots is twice the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots in the first quadrant.\n\nGiven the symmetry and the fact that the polynomial is of degree 48, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots is:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| = 24.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{24}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{24}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:116"}
